gs through the  pipeline so many of you are familiar  with schematic similar to this showing  sort of the timeline that it takes to  get drugs through the therapeutic  pipeline and so it might take four to  six years to screen to do say a  high-throughput screen where you'd be  looking at you know five to ten thousand  different compounds through those high  throughput screens you might narrow your  drug targets down to a couple hundred  you would then go back and start doing  some preclinical testing whether it  would be on human cell lines or in  animal models and only after about five  to seven years have you narrowed these  down often time you narrow it down like  we did in the case of lke spent a few  years studying that molecule on  long-term animal studies only to  discover that it doesn't work and so now  you're back at the drawing board before  you would even go into a clinical trial  which in many cases can take six to  seven years to actually move a drug to  market and so we wanted to come up with  an approach where we could actually move  this faster so one way in terms of the  molecules is that we've developed a very  rapid high content screening platform  for moving drugs through in vitro  studies and the short term in vivo  before we would even take them in to  long term and so at each step of this  process we're eliminating drug compounds  and and for the balance of these studies  were really focused on things that are  already approved  FDA compounds or things that are in the  approval pipeline we then screen those  in either mouth lines patient IPS lines  or patient fibroblasts lines and then go  into sort of short-term pathology to  make sure that it's working before we  would ever go into long term drug  studies and by doing this we're able to  eliminate a lot of compounds but  essentially the way that this high  content screening works is you can look  at a number of different parameters and  screen a number of different drugs so  this is just a sampling of the drugs  that we've been looking at over the last  couple of years but essentially you can  look at many many different parameters  so in this case we're looking at a top  at you using a site OID we're looking at  lysosomal pH using lysis sensor we can  look at mitochondrial function in these  cells we can look at cell rocks to give  an indication of reactive oxygen species  for some reason I have toffee G twice  but you can also use high content  screening to you to look at antibody  based or ICC in these patient cell lines  looking at things like the placement of  the lysosomes you can look at apoptosis  you can look at cell proliferation and  then do other assays on top of this  looking at things like mitochondrial  function in a seahorse as they are  looking at cell stress so it really  allows us to look at a number of  different parameters we don't confine  ourselves just to patient cell lines  like fibroblasts we can also look at  neurons and look at the complexity of  these neurons in terms of axonal  specification and outgrowth in response  to drugs and look at a number of  different parameters like look at  synapse numbers and look at unique cell  populations there's a number of factors  that I think that we often ignore when  we're doing high-throughput or high  content screening Aryans even in vitro  assays that I think that these high  content assays really allow us to  dissect so I'm just going to list a  number of these things that we're  thinking about looking at now whether  there's genotype or phenotype  relationships so do different mutations  even in the same gene  two different types of phenotypic  changes are there sex differences so  imbalance disease I can tell you that we  often do see male and female differences  but at the level of the animal  scientists often disregard that  particularly when they're doing cell  culture are there differences in disease  state and progression so with those  different genetic mutations can we see  changes in the disease status and  progression over time I'm going to see  my mouse the the relevance of the ease  of the assay itself so the high content  can give us very reliable reproducible  results that we can rapidly move through  a number of compounds clearly fairly  quickly and then it also allows us to  ask questions about things that are  actual disease indicators versus  functional attributes of the disease and  then we can use these high-content  assays to start layering drugs together  much more rapidly so instead of doing  drug screenings sequentially we can  actually start to put these together so  just in the last few minutes I want to  tell you about sort of our one of our  big success stories in coming up with  this more rapid model and this was  really when we were looking at all of  those different modalities we knew that  gene therapy probably had the likely the  the highest likelihood of getting us to  the finish line the fastest while we  were continuing to look at a number of  those other molecules so we partnered  with the team at Nationwide Children's  Hospital that have been very successful  in developing an AAV 9 treatment for  spinal muscular atrophy they would have  been able to take this av9 SMA through  clinical trials and we're seeing very  promising results in the kids so what we  decided to do is actually piggyback onto  their successes so we package the seal  and six human gene into their AAA v9  plasmid that has a chicken beta-actin  promoter so it's going to give us very  robust expression throughout the central  nervous system there was some indication  that over expression of some of the NCL  proteins might be site of toxic to the  cells so we started off by just  expressing this plasmid in non-neuronal  cells looking to see if it interfered  with self survive  people you can see here that after RNA  and the protein level in these cells  they look completely healthy and they're  able to produce large amounts of ceylon  six if we left or poor ate this plasmid  into the brain of an embryonic day 15  old mouse and allow that animal to  continue to develop out to postnatal day  7 what you can see when we Co electro /  8 GFP with the human seal and six  plasmid is that we get very nice  expression of ceylon 6 in neurons in the  cortical plate it extends into the  processes similar to what we had seen in  our culture model so just looking here  in the red comparing that to the gfp  colo labeled cells what you can see is  that there appears to be no toxic  effects so over expression of these  proteins or this protein in particular  doesn't really seem to have any toxicity  in the animals and and so we move  forward with injecting this into the  mice for an efficacy study the tropism  of a av9 changes slightly between mouse  and non-human primates and humans so in  these particular studies we give a  single inter cerebral injection of the  AAV 9 starting at we give that injection  at postnatal day 1 and then take these  animals out as long as they will live  and so through that intracranial  injection we get nice increase in RNA  expression of ceylon 6 throughout the  central nervous system all the way down  the spinal cord so this virus is able to  spread very nicely if we look at in situ  hybridization for ceylon 6 in the bottom  row you're looking at a probe specific  for ceiling 6 and you can see nice  spread of this virus or RNA expression  of this from this virus throughout  different regions of the central nervous  system and here is really kind of the  the most telling slide so this is using  in the bottom and antibody that is  specific to the human ceylon 6 and this  is in teal and six mutant mice compared  to a PBS injected control animal on the  top you can see really nice robust  expression of ceylon 6 throughout the  central nervous system  all the brain regions that we looked at  from that single ICV injection so what  we've done over time is look at sort of  the recovery of 