k are the  gray charcoal bars are staying together  the same with if you look on the middle  and left hand side we're looking at  Morris water maze as a measure of  learning and memory the mutant animals  are have an inability as time goes on to  find a platform for Morris water maze  but the mutant animals that are treated  with gene therapy are really on par with  with the wild-type animals probably at  the later stages they're starting to  have a little bit of declining you can  see a slight difference but really it's  substantially better than what we see  for the mutant animals their body weight  is maintained they're healthy they're  happy in those later stages at 12 and 18  months of age just even comparing the  wild-type to the mutant animal are the  sorry gene therapy treated mutants to  the wild-type animals they're really  indistinguishable they're they're  grooming they're walking they're  behaving they're very similar to the  mutant animals that you can see from  these class being scores but really this  is kind of the big take-home message is  that the mutant animals are that solid  blue bar that drops off these are the  untreated animals and as I said they're  dead by 12 to 14 months of age but the  survival of the gene therapy treated  animals in the light blue bars is really  on par and these animals are surviving  out to 24 months of age so probably one  of the the best long-term treatment  approaches that we've seen so the next  step of course would be a clinical trial  so what we were able to do in this  particular study is that in  collaboration with the team at  Nationwide we stacked all of those  things that we would normally do in a  very linear process on top of one  another so instead of doing efficacy  studies waiting for those results and  then doing toxicology studies waiting  for those results and then injecting the  virus into non-human primates  essentially building on the success that  nationwide had had we put those whole  processes on top of each other we were  simultaneously injecting non-human  primates at the same time we were  collecting efficacy data which allowed  us to go to the FDA within eight months  of starting the study and receive IND  approval to start a clinical trial that  clinical trial is ongoing at Nationwide  Hospital we've completed the  or they've completed the early phases of  this which enrolled six patients and now  they're looking to expand inclusion in  that clinical trial that's a phase one  phase two safety trial that's that's  currently ongoing so stay tuned to see  whether this is is working but it really  sort of that accelerated pipeline  drawing on previous expertise and  knowledge has really allowed us to move  this pipeline along a lot faster so just  to summarize I kind of go back to this  slide we have a number of ongoing  studies that are continuing to look at  other treatment modalities now kind of  taking the approach that if the gene  therapy continues to be successful what  will ultimately need to be able to do is  offer potentially other drugs in  combination with gene therapy so instead  of even screening these alone or maybe a  couple of drugs at a time we also  conduct these studies in combination  with gene therapy because there's a lot  of unknowns about gene therapy still we  don't know how long the virus will last  hopefully it will last the lifetime of  these children but will we have to have  something else in our arsenal that's  ready to go should the gene therapy  start to wane over time so I'll just  wrap up here I'll show my research team  really a lot of the gene therapy work  that I highlighted was led by Jacob Kane  a postdoc in the lab and two technicians  Katie white and Derek Tim the basic  biology work was spearheaded by another  postdoc in the lab so neo Co and one of  the graduate students John Rudnick  that's helping her finish up those  studies the gray foundation team I've  shown here in the middle the these folks  have been absolutely instrumental in the  work that we've been able to move  forward and that we continue to do our  creme two collaborators and lastly I  just want to recognize our support that  we receive both from the NIH and from a  number of battens of these foundations  and nf1 foundations thank you very much  thank you dr. Jill Eimer for that  informative presentation we will now  start the lac Q&amp;A portion of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question drop down  box located on the far left of your  presentation window and type your  questions into the box that appears on  your screen  and click the send button will answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for let's get started  our first question is what attracted you  to rare disease research I think there's  a number of things that I find  attractive about rare disease research  one of them is sort of the the limited  focus that's been paid to these diseases  over time so even though every single  rare disease is individually rare  there's always over 7,000 rare diseases  and often times you know it over a  decade ago a lot of the focus was really  on the more prevalent diseases like  Alzheimer's or Parkinson's disease but a  lot of scientists are realizing that  that we can glean a lot of information  from rare diseases both on the basic  biology and therapeutic development side  of things so oftentimes we we look at  this as if we can get a drug approved  and to potentially to the market for a  rare disease that same technology or  even possibly even that same drug might  have applications for these more  prevalent diseases like Alzheimer's or  Parkinson's or other neurodegenerative  diseases so I think that part of it is  sort of the compassion for the patients  that there's nobody are very few people  that are fighting their fight for them  but two on the on the flip side from the  scientific side that we could have a  larger impact by 